Event handler functions can take an additional option
```JS
someNode.addEventListener("click", (e) => {   });
```
`e` Has a variety of properties and methods
**example (see version 3)**
```html
<ul id=list></ul>
```
```JS
const list = document.querySelector("#list");
const data = ["home", "browse", "context"]
for (let d of data) {
	const li = document.createElement("li");
	li.textContent = d;

	//Answer to question 2 below:
	//Set up event handler
	//Version 1:
	li.addEventHandler("click", () => {
			msg.textContent = li.textContent
		});
	//Or version 2:
	li.addEventListener("click", function() {
			msg.textContent = this.textContent;
			//Note that this reffers to li in this context
		});
	//Or version 3, using an event object: 
	//Version 3 is preffered if you have a long event handler !!!
	li.addEventListenor("click", handler(e));
	//end answer

	list.appendChild(li);
}
